@@ -105,6 +105,22 @@ Events cover resource discovery, sessions, agent and message lifecycle, provider
 
 `message_end` can replace a finalized message while preserving its role. `tool_call` can mutate input or block execution. `tool_result` handlers compose, with each handler seeing prior changes.
 
+For final local Bash approval, return `{ admission: { isValid, evaluate, approve } }`
+from `tool_call` for every guarded invocation of the SDK's actual standard-local
+Bash owner, including the configured default. Do not register a replacement merely
+to enable admission. `requireAdmission: true` additionally rejects direct legacy
+calls without context; it is not necessary for dispatcher eligibility. Final `evaluate` may allow a safe plan without UI, hard-deny it, or
+require confirmation, including when prefix/spawnHook changed a harmless raw command. The request
+survives later nonblocking handlers; multiple requests must all affirm, and any
+hard block still wins. The host rejects unknown actual executors before invoking
+them. `approve` runs at the final native local-process boundary, not at this event's
+argument snapshot. See [Final local Bash admission](sdk.md#final-local-bash-admission)
+for signatures, actual-function scope/binding, context-loss handling and the native
+trust boundary. Guarded lost/mismatched context fails before effects, while ordinary
+calls on the same default instance remain compatible before and after guarded use.
+Nested non-admission dispatch explicitly clears native scope and restores its caller;
+concurrent calls cannot borrow each other's frames.
+
 <a id="provider_stream_event"></a>
 
 `provider_stream_event` fires for each parsed provider stream event before Pi normalizes it. The event identifies the provider, API, and model; `event.data` is the earliest structured value available to Pi, not necessarily the original HTTP bytes or SSE frame. Treat it as read-only because mutation can affect normalization. The event is notification-only and is not persisted.
